AB  - This study presents a robust and uncertainty-aware framework for classifying knee radiographs into Normal, Osteopenia, and Osteoporosis categories. Building upon a pre-trained Swin Transformer backbone augmented with Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) blocks, the model is fine-tuned using staged unfreezing and calibrated via vector temperature scaling, isotonic regression. To enhance predictive reliability, the pipeline incorporates Monte Carlo Dropout for epistemic uncertainty estimation and energy-based out-of-distribution (OOD) detection to flag ambiguous or unfamiliar samples during inference. Performance evaluation reveals that the model maintains precision between 81% and 93%, recall above 79%, and F1 scores between 83% and 89%, along with an overall classification accuracy exceeding 90% across all classes. Calibration metrics such as Expected Calibration Error (ECE = 0.0731 & 0.0830) and Maximum Calibration Error (MCE = 0.1474 & 0.1481) using Isotonic and Beta Calibration respectively demonstrate improved confidence alignment after calibration. Furthermore, empirical coverage of 86.67% using conformal prediction underscores the model's reliability in clinical decision-making scenarios. This uncertainty-aware approach ensures not only accurate classification but also reliable confidence estimation, rendering it well-suited for integration into practical medical imaging workflows where interpretability, safety, and robustness are critical.  © 2025 IEEE.
