AB  - Deep learning models for time-series analysis have become foundational in domains such as healthcare monitoring, industrial diagnostics, and risk forecasting. However, the current methods of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) for deep learning models applied to time series problems are fragile under distribution shifts and explainable AI methods can be causally inconsistent, unstable, and are often found to give different predictions from the explanations given. To overcome these challenges, we propose a unified framework for supporting reliable temporal explainability using a combination of causal intervention, shift-invariant regularization (explanation level) of the causal shifts, and mechanistic confirmation via Internal Ablation. This method enforces temporal stability directly on the mechanism of the revised predictive models of the deep learning time series method, rather than at the predictive model level as conventional XAI post-hoc methods do. The proposed framework demonstrates the most favorable results on benchmark datasets: University of East Anglia (UEA) multivariate time series, MIMIC-IV, and PhysioNet 2019 Sepsis. The framework achieves a 19 relative increase (RankCorr = 0.82 vs. 0.69) in explanation stability, improved faithfulness (Deletion Area Under the Curve (AUC) = 0.322 ), and stronger mechanistic alignment (Δ prediction = 0.287), while maintaining competitive in-distribution performance (Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC) = 0.914 vs. 0.912 baseline). The framework increases out-of-distribution AUROC from 0.753 to 0.789 under temporal shifts. These results indicate that solely predictive robustness cannot guarantee reliability to explanations. By establishing and jointly optimizing causal validity, the non-stationary stability, and the internal mechanistic consistency, this work establishes the justification-level robustness as an imperative requirement for reliable temporal AI systems operationalized in safety-critical contexts. This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
