AD  - Center for Astronautical Physics and Engineering, National Central University, 300, Zhongda Rd., Zhongli District, Taoyuan City, 320317, Taiwan
AD  - Global Atmospheric Observations and Data Applications Research Center, National Central University, 300, Zhongda Rd., Zhongli District, Taoyuan City, 320317, Taiwan
AB  - Small satellite platforms, such as CubeSats, increasingly seek higher data throughput while operating under stringent size, weight, and power constraints. In compact software-defined radio (SDR) receivers, conventional demodulation chains built from carrier recovery, matched filtering, timing synchronization, and hard decisions can require frequent retuning but still suffer from waveform distortion caused by band-limiting, residual oscillator impairments such as sampling frequency offset (SFO) and timing offset (TO), and inter-symbol interference (ISI). In this study, we propose a deep-learning approach for Quadrature Phase Shift Keying (QPSK) demodulation based on a long short-term memory (LSTM) architecture, and develop an end-to-end evaluation process for channels with additive noise, ISI, and synchronization impairments. Rather than relying on explicit matched filtering and dedicated timing-alignment preprocessing, the proposed receiver processes raw passband waveform segments directly under the evaluated AWGN + ISI + TO + SFO setting. We adopt a causal sliding-window sequence formulation that preserves multi-sample temporal context, enabling the model to learn ISI-driven dependencies and mitigate uncompensated offsets for symbol-wise classification. An architectural grid search identifies that a lightweight configuration of 1 hidden layer and 32 hidden units offers a favorable trade-off between demodulation accuracy and training cost. To improve robustness in low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) regions, we apply curriculum learning with exponential learning-rate decay, progressively training the model from high to low SNR. Simulations show that our lightweight LSTM-based demodulator outperforms both standard matched-filter and minimum mean-squared-error decision-feedback equalizer (MMSE-DFE) baselines under combined impairment conditions, and converges more stably under the curriculum framework. Furthermore, the LSTM maintains robust performance under out-of-distribution testing with unseen channel variations and exhibits substantially lower 90th- and 95th-percentile symbol error rate (SER) values, suggesting a practical option for SDR-based satellite payloads and ground receivers operating across varying sampling rates and SNR scenarios. © 2026 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
