AB  - Deep learning offers promising solutions for medical image analysis, but its generalizability across different clinical environments remains challenging. This challenge is often due to the variability in data distribution and the limited availability of comprehensive annotated datasets, which can lead to models performing poorly on out-of-distribution datasets. This paper introduces a multi-modal framework that enhances the generalizability of medical imaging models by integrating radiology reports. Our approach utilizes a CNN-based visual encoder and transformers to contrast radiographic images and reports. We demonstrate significant performance improvements on out-of-distribution datasets through rigorous cross-dataset evaluations. To address the scarcity of professional medical image-text pairs, we propose a gradual knowledge transfer strategy that incrementally enhances model performance. Experimental results indicate that our method significantly improves generalization, with AUC values increasing from 0.735 to 0.880 and 0.609 to 0.811 on two out-of-distribution test datasets. Attention maps provide visual explanations that validate the model's alignment with radiological insights. Copyright © 2024 held by the owner/author(s).
