AD  - School of Computing and Artificial Intelligence, Southwest Jiaotong University, Sichuan Province, Chengdu, 611756, China
AB  - Multimodal intent recognition requires interpreting complex human behavior from heterogeneous signals, but researchers often overlook the fundamental modeling conflict when trying to understand noisy sequences under resource constraints. Specifically, existing methods struggle to reconcile modality heterogeneity with semantic alignment, as well as global temporal trends with local transient details. To address these challenges, inspired by the human cognitive process of Perception–Comprehension–Induction, we establish an explicit stage-to-module mapping that guides the design of each component, resulting in a unified text-centric paradigm for multimodal intent recognition. We propose a Text-Guided Frequency-Decoupled (TGFD) framework as a concrete instantiation of this paradigm, providing a systematic blueprint for resolving conflicts between modality heterogeneity and feature granularity. TGFD ensures that dominant textual semantics guide the processing of noisy, multi-granular auxiliary signals. Within perception phase, we introduce Text-Semantics Guided Gating (TSGG), which enforces a text-centric inductive bias by constraining non-verbal representations to a semantically consistent subspace for noise suppression. Within comprehension phase, we propose the Locality-Augmented State Space Model (LASSM), whose frequency-decoupled temporal modeling reconciles long-range semantic trends with local intent triggers. Finally, within induction phase, we introduce Prototype-Anchored Alignment (PAA) as a structured regularization strategy to mitigate feature sparsity in small-batch training. Extensive experiments on MIntRec and MIntRec 2.0 demonstrate that TGFD outperforms state-of-the-art methods and exhibits superior generalization in Out-of-Distribution (OOD) scenarios. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
