AD  - School of Computer Science, South China Normal University, Guangzhou, 510631, China
AB  - Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection remains challenging because neural networks may assign high confidence to anomalous inputs, especially when in-distribution (ID) features are not well separated. This issue is particularly problematic for prototype-guided methods, whose scores depend on reliable sample-to-prototype alignment. To address this limitation, we propose an OOD detection framework that combines similarity-weighted energy scoring with uncertainty-aware prototype refinement. Specifically, we stabilize class prototypes through momentum-based updating, regularize the feature space with prototype-oriented constraints, and use Joint Uncertainty Quantification (JUQ) to downweight ambiguous or noisy samples during training. Experiments on CIFAR and ImageNet benchmarks show consistent improvements over the closest prototype-aware baseline, while maintaining competitive performance among representative OOD detection methods. Additional analyses further indicate improved prototype alignment, larger prototype gaps, and reduced similarity-distribution overlap between ID and OOD samples. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
