TI  - Meta-CAMAF: A meta-learning enhanced context-aware multimodal alignment framework for explainable risk stratification in lung diseases
PY  - 2026
T2  - Array
VL  - 30
C7  - 100908
DO  - 10.1016/j.array.2026.100908
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038921697?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Computer Science and Engineering (SCOPE), VIT-AP University, Andhra Pradesh, India
AB  - Lung diseases remain a critical global health challenge, requiring accurate and trustworthy risk stratification to support clinical decision-making. While multimodal deep learning has shown promise, existing models often struggle with heterogeneous data integration, rare disease generalization, and interpretability for end-users. To address these challenges, we propose Meta-CAMAF, a meta-learning enhanced context-aware multimodal alignment framework that integrates TabTransformer for electronic health records and Vision Transformer (ViT) for chest X-rays. A context encoder and dynamic alignment layer generate patient-specific representations, while a meta-context gating mechanism enables rapid adaptation to rare disease cases in few-shot settings. Beyond predictive accuracy, Meta-CAMAF provides attention-based interpretability for visualizing modality contributions and highlighting clinically relevant features, strengthening its role as a decision-support tool. Evaluations on CheXpert, MIMIC-IV, and PadChest datasets show state-of-the-art performance with an accuracy of 0.9736, F1-score of 0.9698, and AUC-ROC of 0.9864, significantly outperforming recent multimodal and meta-learning baselines. Robustness analyses under noise, calibration diagnostics, out-of-distribution detection, and statistical significance testing confirm the reliability and clinical viability of the framework. Meta-CAMAF thus offers a scalable and explainable solution for precision healthcare, bridging technical advances in multimodal learning with real-world clinical deployment needs. © 2026
