AD  - School of Computing and Artificial Intelligence, Fuyao University of Science and Technology, No. 104, Zhihui Avenue, High-tech Industrial Development District, Fujian, Fuzhou, 350109, China
AB  - High-quality segmentation and reliable uncertainty estimation are both essential for trustworthy medical image analysis, yet existing methods typically treat them as loosely coupled objectives. In this work, we revisit this paradigm and identify a structural limitation in U-shaped architectures, namely the feature–semantic misalignment, where intermediate representations simultaneously encode spatial details and evolving semantic abstractions, leading to inconsistent confidence estimation and degraded calibration under distribution shifts. To address this issue, we propose AUC-Net, a principled framework that explicitly models the interaction between representation alignment and uncertainty estimation. Unlike conventional attention mechanisms that primarily enhance feature saliency, we introduce the Intermediate-Perception Attention Module (IPAM), which performs top-down semantic-conditioned reweighting of mid-level features, enabling progressive alignment between categorical semantics and spatial structures. Building upon this aligned representation, we further develop a dual-objective uncertainty formulation that integrates evidential deep learning with a topology-aware segmentation loss. Specifically, a Dirichlet-based evidential formulation captures data uncertainty, while a Softplus-smoothed Log-Dice term regularizes boundary consistency, jointly improving both prediction reliability and structural fidelity. Extensive experiments on four benchmark datasets (ISIC2018, ACDC, LiTS2017, and MVSeg-3D TEE) demonstrate that AUC-Net achieves consistent improvements in calibration and robustness, particularly under out-of-distribution perturbations. These results suggest that explicitly addressing representation-level misalignment provides a complementary pathway to improving both segmentation accuracy and uncertainty reliability. The code will be available at https://github.com/zhangjy2008327/AUC-Net. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
