UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036252460?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Artificial Intelligence, Chung-Ang University, Dongjak-gu, Seoul, 06974, South Korea
AB  - Diffusion models are attractive for out-of-distribution (OOD) detection, but most prior diffusion-based detectors rely on pixel-space signals that require repeated denoiser evaluations across noise levels, increasing latency and often exhibiting sensitivity to input complexity. We introduce an efficient, unsupervised alternative that repurposes a pretrained unconditional diffusion backbone as a frozen representation extractor. From a test image, we extract intermediate diffusion features at a chosen noise level and network depth, and perform OOD detection using Mahalanobis scoring, thereby avoiding iterative pixel-space integration-based scoring. Because representation-based OOD performance varies substantially across extraction timesteps and network blocks, we further introduce an ID-only operating-point selection protocol that instantiates a single extraction setting from unlabeled in-distribution statistics alone. Our analyses show that discriminability proxies (e.g., linear probing) are insufficient to reliably explain or select the best operating region, and that stable OOD scoring is better understood by jointly considering multiple aspects of compatibility with covariance-based Mahalanobis scoring in the extracted feature distribution, including covariance conditioning, effective rank, and Gaussianity-related tail behavior. Building on this perspective, we additionally evaluate REPresentation Alignment (REPA), which aligns intermediate diffusion features to a frozen self-supervised encoder (e.g., DINOv2), and find that it systematically improves these distributional diagnostics and yields consistent AUROC gains under the same Mahalanobis scoring pipeline. Experiments under a fixed-ID multi-OOD benchmark using both U-Net and Transformer diffusion backbones demonstrate strong OOD detection with a substantial efficiency advantage over iterative diffusion scoring baselines. ©2026 The Authors.
