AD  - China University of Petroleum (East China), Qingdao Institute of Software, College of Computer Science and Technology, Qingdao, China
AD  - Shanghai Jiao Tong University, School of Aeronautics and Astronautics, Shanghai, China
AB  - Bearing fault diagnosis is crucial for ensuring the reliability of rotating machinery. While recent advances in deep learning have demonstrated promising results, existing approaches remain susceptible to domain shifts and often produce overconfident predictions on out-of-distribution inputs. To address these issues, we propose Reliable BearLLM, a knowledgeinformed and distance-calibrated framework for robust bearing fault diagnosis. The proposed framework integrates vibration signals and user prompts, enabling flexible human-in-the-loop inference. A domain knowledge-informed feature alignment module, leveraging frequency analysis techniques, captures domaininvariant features to mitigate feature shift across different operating conditions. Additionally, a distance-calibrated classifier is designed to provide diagnostic results with interpretable confidence scores. Experiments across different working conditions and datasets demonstrate that Reliable BearLLM outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both diagnostic accuracy and out-of-distribution detection robustness. The proposed framework bridges LLMs with industrial diagnostics, offering a scalable and trustworthy solution for real-world equipment health monitoring.  © 2025 IEEE.
