AB  - Oriented object detection (OOD) serves as a foundational research area within the field of remote sensing image analysis, with significant application value in disaster management and surveillance. The detection transformer employs one-to-one matching to achieve the end-to-end object detection, drawing increasing attention in the field of oriented object detection. However, the existing transformer-based OOD methods often ignore the misalignment between classification scores and localization quality in training. This misalignment can lead to predictions with high localization quality being treated as negative samples in one-to-one matching. In addition, misalignment between classification and localization tasks may cause discrepancies in the optimization of loss functions, leading to inefficient network training. To solve these problems, we introduce the QA-DETR method, an oriented detection transformer designed to achieve quality alignment between classification and localization. The method comprises two key components: a quality-aligned cost (QAC) and a quality-aligned loss (QAL). Specifically, the quality-aligned cost is determined by employing various joint representation of classification and localization at different training duration. This approach ensures that samples with high localization quality are recognized as positive samples during one-to-one matching. The quality-aligned loss leverages the localization metrics to supervise the classification prediction scores, ensuring consistency in the optimization of both classification and localization during training. Our method achieves superior performance on the DOTA dataset, outperforming existing transformer-based OOD methods with fewer epochs. © 2025 IEEE.
