TI  - Color space aware synchronous and asynchronous transformer fusions for classification of ER biomarker in breast cancer IHC images
PY  - 2026
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
VL  - 118
C7  - 109756
DO  - 10.1016/j.bspc.2026.109756
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105029084137?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Computer Science and Information Technology, Cotton University, Assam, Guwahati, India
AD  - Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Memphis, Memphis, TN, United States
AD  - Biomedical Sensors and Systems Lab, University of Memphis, Memphis, TN, United States
AD  - North East Cancer Hospital and Research Institute, Assam, Guwahati, India
AB  - Estrogen Receptor (ER) biomarker assessment in breast cancer via Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is critical factor for guiding treatment decisions. While deep learning has shown promise in automating this task, domain shifts caused by color variability across samples continue to limits model generalizability. The influence of diverse color space representations in conjunction with vision transformers remains underexplored in this context. In this study, we proposed a color space-aware transformer fusion framework for ER expression classification, investigating the joint contribution of color space transformations, transformer architectures and embedding fusion architectures. We benchmark five state-of-the-art transformer models: Swin Transformer V2, EdgeNeXt, TinyViT, MobileViT, and PVTv2, across six color spaces: RGB, LAB, HSL, HED, HSV, and YCbCr. Using Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis (MCDA), we identify LAB and HSL as the most discriminative color spaces and Swin Transformer V2 and EdgeNeXt as the best-performing architectures. Based on these insights, we design two fusion architectures: Synchronous Fusion using homogeneous backbones and Asynchronous Fusion using heterogeneous backbones. Both architectures incorporate a Color Attention Fusion (CAF) module and a cosine similarity loss to enforce semantic alignment between color-modality embeddings. Extensive evaluation on test and out-of-distribution (OOD) sets shows that the Asynchronous Fusion model with cosine similarity achieves the highest performance (accuracy: 0.9104 test, 0.9176 OOD). Embedding cluster quality metrics (Silhouette Score, Davies–Bouldin Index, Calinski–Harabasz Index) and visualization tools (Grad-CAM, UMAP) further validate the enhanced feature compactness and domain robustness. This work demonstrates the synergistic potential of multi-color space fusion with transformers for robust and interpretable ER biomarker classification. © 2026
